{
  "story_id": "S066",
  "title": "TBL1XR1 and GSK3B perturbations define distinct Wnt-adjacent thalamic states instead of a canonical beta-catenin cascade",
  "status": "grounded",
  "rejection_reason": null,
  "exact_observed_result": "Tbl1xr1 generated a compact 151 TH Prkcd Grin2c Glut signature with Vangl1 logFC -0.422 FDR 0.00483, Prkcg -0.235 FDR 0.00483, Rasa4 -0.550 FDR 0.0288, and Kctd16 0.354 FDR 0.0288. Ctnnb1 and Tcf7l2 perturbations in the same cell type had much larger signatures but near-zero Pearson correlations with Tbl1xr1. Gsk3b was depleted in the same 151 TH class yet did not induce canonical Axin2/Lef1 output like Apc perturbation.",
  "biological_interpretation": "TBL1XR1 and GSK3B sit near Wnt/beta-catenin biology in the literature, but in differentiated thalamic neurons their knockdown states are sparse and target-specific rather than a simple canonical beta-catenin transcriptional cascade.",
